WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:02.320
Film Courage: When should a writer quit their
full-time job?

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:06.200
Erik Bork, Screenwriter/Author: Only when
writing has become one that can replace that

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:14.089
full-time job basically I would say which
you kind of know it when it happens I think.

00:00:14.089 --> 00:00:18.980
Obviously if you have a full time job because
you need that amount of income and you can’t

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:24.119
get it if you quit because writing isn’t
bringing it then I probably wouldn’t be

00:00:24.119 --> 00:00:25.119
quitting.

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:26.369
I think most people tend to get it.

00:00:26.369 --> 00:00:31.960
I don’t see people quitting their jobs even
when they have a professional nibble at writing

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:34.080
because you can have a nibble that doesn’t
turn into anything.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.340
Even if you get an agent, even if you get
a job, even if you get a job on writing staff

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:42.410
of a hit show that could be over in six months
and you may not get another one.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:46.640
You’re going to suspend your full-time day
job while you’re on that hit show because

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:50.430
you probably have to go to work every day
on the show at the office you know with the

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:56.360
rest of the writers but I would say it’s
great if you have other ways to earn a living

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:03.570
that you like and that have growth potential
and no reason to let those go but there may

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:08.520
come a time where you have to stop because
I’m writing on a movie and they need me

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:12.329
full-time for the next six months and they’re
paying me half a million dollars or whatever.

00:01:12.329 --> 00:01:15.540
Film Courage: That would be a good problem
to have.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.740
What about the notion that people have where
I just need six months, I’m going to take

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:21.759
this time off and this is going to go great.

00:01:21.759 --> 00:01:24.619
I’ll get up everyday and I have this regimen,
this practice.

00:01:24.619 --> 00:01:26.670
I’m going to crank these pages out.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:28.249
Do you think this really works?

00:01:28.249 --> 00:01:31.479
Or do you think it’s this romantic version
of writing?

00:01:31.479 --> 00:01:33.400
Erik: I don’t know if it works.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:34.820
I’ve never really done that.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:37.119
It maybe works sometimes for some people.

00:01:37.119 --> 00:01:41.130
I am more of a believer that you carve out
time in your regular life every day or like

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:42.130
five days a week.

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:44.299
So if that means getting up early for an hour.

00:01:44.299 --> 00:01:49.850
I mean Ron Bass used to be a lawyer and the
story was that he would wake up at 3:00 a.m.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:53.240
and write for four hours and then go and be
an entertainment lawyer all day back before

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:55.200
he was a famous screenwriter.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:00.240
But even an hour at night, an hour at lunch,
whatever, half hour, every Saturday and Sunday

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:05.829
for two hours each and then although that
I think is not as good as five days a week

00:02:05.829 --> 00:02:10.440
but the idea of I’m going to take this time
off and then when this time is over, I don’t

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:13.760
have time to write anymore I don’t think
tends to work as well.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.420
That’s not to say you can’t take a retreat
for a week or a month.

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:21.810
I think John August does that, he goes to
a hotel in Las Vegas to pump out a first draft

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:26.360
in three weeks or whatever like a change of
scenery or getting away from the normal daily

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:31.920
responsibilities can work but I think it works
best combined with you also have a daily writing

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.950
practice and you’re moving it forward every
day in some way as well.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:41.830
Film Courage: Do you think the notion of wanting
to take this long stretch of time out is actually

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:44.610
damaging because you lose touch with the day-to-day?

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:49.900
Because when you’re not working or you are
on vacation your mind is not in the reality

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.260
of the day-to-day.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:54.300
Your stressors are different.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:58.780
You’re not worrying about being on someone
else’s time schedule which you are when

00:02:58.780 --> 00:02:59.780
you have a job?

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:02.380
You are at the mercy of their schedule pretty
much.

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:05.080
Do you lose touch with writing [when this
happens]?

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:08.620
Erik: When you’re saying when you go off
and write and you leave your regular life

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.750
will your writing not be as good because you’re
out of touch with regular life?

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:12.750
Film Courage: Pretty much, yes.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.480
Erik: I don’t really see that as an issue.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.950
I think it’s okay to not be currently immersed
in your normal life to be able to write.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:23.080
I think it’s just more a danger that just
somebody never writes until they have that

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:26.810
magical stretch of time where they think they
are going to write all day, every day and

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:28.290
it’s going to be great.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:32.030
I think most writers who are professionals
tend to more have a block of time they do

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:36.690
it every day to maybe a shorter block but
they are always kind of doing it and moving

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:37.690
it forward.

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:37.702
Question For The Viewers: When should a writer
quit their day job?

